WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.592
Say nginx serves your web page, and writes every request to a log file.

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.980
You want a helper that reads each new line of that log.

3
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:15.259
It only works right next to the app, because it must read the app's files.

4
00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:18.283
Kubernetes has a wrapper for this, the Pod.

5
00:00:18.283 --> 00:00:21.688
One or more containers, placed on one node together.

6
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.010
They share one IP address and one set of ports.

7
00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.524
So they reach each other on localhost.

8
00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:29.694
They can also share volumes.

9
00:00:29.694 --> 00:00:31.585
Here, a folder called logs.

10
00:00:31.585 --> 00:00:35.156
nginx writes into it, and the helper reads from it.

11
00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:36.826
And they share one life.

12
00:00:36.826 --> 00:00:40.415
They start on the same node, and they are removed together.

13
00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:42.963
nginx also needs a page to serve.

14
00:00:42.963 --> 00:00:49.671
In the lab's example Pod, a third container writes that page into a second folder, called work.

15
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:56.567
The manifest lists two init containers, write-page and log-tailer, and one app container, nginx.

16
00:00:56.567 --> 00:00:57.956
So who starts first?

17
00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:01.538
Watch it start, with kubectl get and the watch flag.

18
00:01:01.538 --> 00:01:04.132
At first, nothing in the Pod is running.

19
00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:07.884
Init containers run first, one at a time, in order.

20
00:01:07.884 --> 00:01:11.643
Each must exit with code 0 before the next one starts.

21
00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:17.864
The first one writes index.html into the work volume, then exits with code 0.

22
00:01:17.864 --> 00:01:20.435
STATUS moves to Init, one of two.

23
00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:24.867
So Kubernetes counts log-tailer as an init container too.

24
00:01:24.867 --> 00:01:27.004
But a log tailer never exits.

25
00:01:27.004 --> 00:01:28.772
Does nginx wait forever?

26
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:29.234
No.

27
00:01:29.234 --> 00:01:34.313
Look at its one extra line in the manifest: restartPolicy Always.

28
00:01:34.313 --> 00:01:36.892
That one line makes it a sidecar.

29
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:42.588
Kubernetes starts a sidecar in init order, then moves on as soon as it is running.

30
00:01:42.588 --> 00:01:44.729
It does not wait for it to exit.

31
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:46.178
Then nginx starts.

32
00:01:46.178 --> 00:01:50.536
READY becomes two of two, because READY counts the sidecar too.

33
00:01:50.767 --> 00:01:55.531
Now ask nginx for its page, from inside the Pod, on localhost.

34
00:01:55.733 --> 00:02:00.840
The answer is the file the init container wrote, before nginx even existed.

35
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.427
It stayed behind in the shared volume.

36
00:02:03.633 --> 00:02:08.347
nginx also wrote that request into access.log, in the logs volume.

37
00:02:08.347 --> 00:02:11.561
The sidecar read the new line and printed it.

38
00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:13.303
Why this order?

39
00:02:13.303 --> 00:02:18.417
Think of the old way, where the helper was just a second normal container.

40
00:02:18.417 --> 00:02:20.905
Each dot is one log line from nginx.

41
00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:24.297
A log shipper that started late missed the first lines.

42
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.109
And one that stopped early lost the last ones.

43
00:02:27.333 --> 00:02:32.627
A native sidecar starts before the app, and stops only after the app has stopped.

44
00:02:32.627 --> 00:02:34.131
So it reads every line.

45
00:02:34.333 --> 00:02:37.860
And if it crashes in between, Kubernetes restarts it.

46
00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:41.032
At shutdown, the order runs backwards.

47
00:02:41.032 --> 00:02:42.436
nginx stops first.

48
00:02:42.436 --> 00:02:44.231
The sidecar stops last.

49
00:02:45.033 --> 00:02:47.904
So when does a helper belong in the same Pod?

50
00:02:47.904 --> 00:02:52.242
Only when the containers must share a node, an IP address, or files.

51
00:02:52.242 --> 00:02:58.493
An app and its database belong in separate Pods, so you can scale and replace each one on its own.

52
00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.438
Init containers finish first.

53
00:03:01.438 --> 00:03:04.244
The sidecar starts next and keeps running.

54
00:03:04.244 --> 00:03:05.581
Then the app starts.

55
00:03:05.581 --> 00:03:08.721
And all of them share the Pod's IP and volumes.
